# The certificate that signs every published APEX Remote APK.
#
# This file is the PUBLISHED value. A person who has downloaded an APK can run
#
#     apksigner verify --print-certs apex-remote-<version>.apk
#
# and compare the "certificate SHA-256 digest" it prints against the line(s)
# below. Nothing secret is here: a certificate fingerprint is a public fact
# about a public key, and publishing it is the only way a stranger can tell
# an APK built by this repository from one built by somebody else.
#
# FORMAT: one fingerprint per line, SHA-256 of the DER certificate, as 64
# lowercase hex characters. `keytool -list -v` prints the same bytes uppercase
# and colon-separated; either spelling is accepted and normalised.
#
# HOW IT IS USED: `android/tools/verify-signing-identity.sh` refuses a release
# whose signers are not EXACTLY this set — before the build, against the
# keystore, and after it, against the APK that would be published. So a
# keystore secret replaced by a different key stops the release instead of
# shipping an APK that no installed copy of the app can be updated from.
#
# WHILE IT SAYS `UNSET` NO RELEASE CAN BE CUT, and that is deliberate: no
# signing key exists yet. `android/tools/generate-signing-key.sh` writes the
# real value here when Andre generates the key. See docs/android-signing.md.
#
# ROTATION: `apksigner rotate` signs with the new key and keeps the old one in
# a SigningCertificateLineage, so a rotated APK legitimately carries TWO
# certificates — measured 2026-09-20 with build-tools 36.0.0: the new key
# covers minSdkVersion 33+ and the old key 24..32. Both fingerprints go here,
# in the same commit as the rotation. docs/android-signing.md has the whole
# procedure.
9b2418f3cd37ba2ae83cdaeec5068280e02dc64135fdb1bb9fcb247326a66c67
